\subsection{Encounter-period phase matching}

The empirical acceleration-amplitude schedule alone can hold
$\tau_a$ at its \SI{0.15}{s} lower bound when a following sea lowers the
acceleration while lengthening the encounter period. The restoring PII
core then has more phase lead relative to the wave, without a matching
increase in acceleration-filter lag.

For the frozen continuous-time core, omitting its separate bias channel,
the transfer from true displacement to reconstructed displacement before
acceleration filtering is
\begin{equation}
 C=\left(\frac{i\omega}{r_{\mathrm{cmd}}+i\omega}\right)^3=A+iB,
 \qquad \omega=2\pi f_f.
\end{equation}
With $x=\omega\tau_a$, the squared reconstruction error is
\begin{equation}
 E(x)=\left|\frac{C}{1+ix}-1\right|^2
 =\frac{(A-1)^2+(B-x)^2}{1+x^2}.
\end{equation}
On the positive-phase branch $A>0$, $B>0$, define
$g=2A-|C|^2$. The positive minimizer has the stable form
\begin{equation}
 x_* = \frac{2B}{g+\sqrt{g^2+4B^2}},\qquad
 \tau_{a,*}=\frac{x_*}{\omega}.
\end{equation}
The implemented command is
\begin{equation}
 \tau_{a,\mathrm{cmd}}=\clamp\!\left(
 \max(\tau_{a,\mathrm{empirical}},\tau_{a,*}),
 \tau_{a,\min},\tau_{a,\max}\right).
\end{equation}
Outside that branch the empirical command is retained. Existing stronger
noise smoothing, the \SIrange{0.15}{0.90}{s} bounds, and the existing
parameter EMA all remain. The already filtered measured frequency supplies
$\omega$; neither simulated wave truth nor vessel speed enters the filter.
The restoring-pole, bias and attitude laws are unchanged.
\texttt{match\_accel\_filter\_phase=false} reproduces the preceding schedule.

This frozen scalar calculation does not guarantee improvement for every
broadband nonlinear execution. Replacing the empirical time constant
outright worsened the largest zero-speed record; retaining its smoothing
floor avoids that loss. The C++ regression checks the complex frequency
response, bounds and an actual sinusoidal reconstruction independently of
the algebra used by the scheduler.

On the exact-clock v1.3.1 zero-speed and \SI{5}{kn} records, one executable
runs both settings with identical default and held-out sensor seeds 7 and
23. The 96 runs give 48 paired comparisons, each scored at full rate over
the final \SI{900}{s}. All 24 moving-record pairs improve. Their mean
normalized heave RMS falls from $12.128$ to $8.734\,\%H_s$; worst error falls
from $15.387$ to $10.487\,\%H_s$. The zero-speed worst remains
$9.148\,\%H_s$ across the three seeds ($8.749\,\%H_s$ on the default seed).
The largest observed zero-speed cost is \SI{0.316}{mm} RMS on a small
PM--Stokes record. Paired attitude metrics are identical. These finite
records are not population confidence intervals.

The complete paired values, input and source hashes, compiler information
and the rejected cutoff studies are retained under
\texttt{reports/results/encounter\_scaling}. The reproduction driver is
\texttt{tools/encounter\_scaling\_study.py}; the model analysis and complete
ablation discussion are in \texttt{docs/encounter-scaling-regressions.md}.
